were weakened (strict host-key checking remained enforced throughout, and the CA private key retained restrictive permissions).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-eff0301f16a7415c\",\"cmd-d8369823479f4b40\",\"cmd-67ba57109f164628\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the task were directly and satisfactorily demonstrated: node1 hosts a new SSH CA, its host key was signed into a certificate, sshd was configured and confirmed to present that certificate, and a client trusting only the CA (via @cert-authority, no pinned host key) connected successfully under strict host-key checking, contrasted against a documented baseline failure without CA trust. The independent restart-evidence protocol was also fully and coherently executed, showing a genuine reboot (boot_id change matching the fixed lifecycle snapshots) with healthy pre- and post-restart sshd/CA state and re-verified CA-based trust afterward. Minor hygiene issues (a failed intermediate reload requiring a corrective restart, and two small leftover temp files) do not affect functional correctness.\",\"limitations\":[\"Trust verification relied on ssh debug1/debug2 log lines and process exit codes rather than an independently reproduced client session outside the executor's own commands.\",\"No test was shown confirming the client would reject a host presenting a certificate NOT signed by the trusted CA (negative-case for certificate validation), though this was not explicitly required by the task instruction.\",\"The brief sshd 'crashed' status after the reload (cmd-eff0301f16a7415c) is unexplained beyond ps/netstat output; root cause of the reload failure was not diagnosed further, only worked around via full restart.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"ca-created","requirement":"Create an SSH certificate authority on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"An ed25519 CA keypair was generated at /etc/ssh/ca/ssh_host_ca(.pub) on node1 via ssh-keygen, c